# Testing discipline

## Bug fixes — test first, always
Write a failing test that reproduces the bug BEFORE writing the fix.
The test proves the bug exists, and proves the fix works. No regression test → the bug will return.

## New behavior with clear inputs/outputs
Write the test (expected inputs → expected outputs) first, then implement until it passes.
This applies to new functions, methods, endpoints, and CLI commands with deterministic behavior.

## Refactors — lock behavior first
Before restructuring, ensure tests cover the existing behavior. If coverage is missing, add characterization tests first, then refactor against them.

## When TDD does NOT apply
Skip test-first for: config-only changes, prototype/spike code, trivial one-liners, pure UI layout/styling, and generated files.
Still write tests after if the code will ship.

## Test quality
- Tests must be independent — no shared mutable state between test cases.
- Tests must be deterministic — no flaky reliance on timing, network, or random values.
- Prefer parametrized / table-driven tests for multiple input-output cases.
- Name tests after the behavior they verify, not the function they call.
